Wrong! The state of an arbitrary wave function is compound of many eigen
functions. When measuring the wave function has different probabilities to
collapses to one of the eigen functions. The sum of the probabilities is unity.

Each particle has its own set of eigen functions. The forces between two
particles also affect the wave functions of them.

A cat has so many particles so that the wave functions create a wave package.
The probability of a cat changing its state when measurement is applied is
very close to zero.

That is why an electron has a reasonable probability of "tunneling" through
high potential barrier (not infinite), but no one so a chair do "tunneling"


